815-8778-a9279ad7fa8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127716 #edb/ref 1080 "$x =-2$ and $x =-1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127716 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127716 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "634c85b3-8ba0-5c3e-8f34-c077b0971d4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128903 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128903 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $y=f(x)$ below, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?\n\n![](images/1a/1aeebec4df41080efc0d91a8adf00a799828e793e731ed7a36d32d881110596b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128903 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "001529aa-c97a-5d69-82fb-cd4319a4dd7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128903 #edb/ref 1074 "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1$. Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}} f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1$. Similarly\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1^{-}} f(x) = \\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=1$.\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2$, but this is a point discontinuity, not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128904 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128904 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all the points where the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-5x+6}$ has infinite discontinuities." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128904 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58f2bf5b-04b1-5931-8452-c095b55722ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128904 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{x^{2} - 5x + 6} \\\\\n&= (1)/((x - 3)(x - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-2) = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=2,3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=2$ and $x=3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128905 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128905 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all the points where the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x^2-4}$ has infinite discontinuities." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128905 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26d318a3-0b3f-50be-9802-92b10b83d119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128905 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} + x - 6}{x^{2} - 4} \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 3}{x + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+2 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=-2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128906 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128906 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n${\\frac{1}{x - 5},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x - 4},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 5$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128906 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83f4e413-e2f4-53dd-9893-fdf8e2df2642" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128906 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$. The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-5}\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-4}\n$$\n\ngrow without bound within their respective subdomains.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-5}$. Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n$x-5=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=5$.\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x < 5$, we do have that\n$\\lim_{x\\to 5^{-}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x-5}\\right) = -\\infty$,\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}$. Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n$x-4=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=4$.\nHowever, this branch of the function is defined for $x \\geq 5$, which does not contain the neighborhood of $x=4$. So $x=4$ cannot be a vertical asymptote, and therefore $x=4$ cannot be an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ only has a single infinite discontinuity, and it is at $x=5$.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1e68cf3a87e981a07fdf6c8a2816ed25b26670b8afa274d95f05e7ea90ee340.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes Using Graphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e3170b49-74e4-540e-bd64-1be5e84605d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "976f6e20-2c45-5123-bb60-738e099facc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759