#edb/ref 1069 "q-38909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129705 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/74/744bb33e33eb84d02f53403b9f6ccd6077f6f9a09b5781ffdc31f61c6b5b8343.png)\n\nFor the function $y = f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129705 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41d68181-b01b-50ec-a85b-6bd02941be55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129905 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129905 #edb/ref 1061 12112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "545e237d-8d82-5fad-8893-b53c598fe510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129906 #edb/ref 1061 12113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8acbfdc6-b18f-5290-b574-bb7515ced757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1061 12114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf945475-7921-5714-a012-fd5bfdd13e6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1061 12115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18da17af-7589-58b8-ac89-6eded53c96a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129909 #edb/ref 1061 12116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129909 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23a64c25-6876-5b6c-9e24-a82e1cee59ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129909 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's take a look at the function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nbelow.\n\n![](images/cc/cc9593860b0c134d0d99635b37eeac4f16a712a7af9e1c6474fcf1f885e8451e.png)\n\nThe function has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$. There is also a discontinuity in the function at $x=0$, since we cannot draw the points on the curve without taking our pen off the paper to cross the asymptote.\n\nSo, we say that\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nhas a **discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote** at $x=0$. Discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes are also called **infinite discontinuities**.\n\nNotice that here, both one-sided limits are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) =-\\infty\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x \\right)=\\infty\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $y=f(x)$ has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=c$ if either one-sided limit is infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c^-} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to c^+} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\n$$\n\nNote that we only need a single limit to become infinite to conclude that there is an infinite discontinuity at $x=c$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1065 2595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ce83f36f-d7f8-546f-b887-1e6818a75bfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
